B.Po=new A.bb("strategy.outposts","Outposts","An outpost is a square in the enemy half that no enemy pawn can ever attack. A knight planted there, protected by a pawn, is often worth more than a rook.","r2q1rk1/pp2bppp/3p4/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPP2PPP/R2Q1RK1 w - - 0 1",B.Q3,null,!1,"","d5",!1,B.C)
B.eQ=s([],t.u)
B.Pj=new A.bb("strategy.weakpawns","Weak pawns","Pawns protect each other only side by side. Three kinds cannot be protected by a neighbour and become targets. Isolated: no friendly pawn on either adjacent file (a2). Doubled: two on one file, the back one stuck (f2 and f3). Backward: its neighbours have advanced past it and it cannot safely follow (c3). Attack them with pieces, and put a piece on the square in front of them.","6k1/pp3ppp/2p5/3p4/3P4/2P2P2/P4P1P/6K1 w - - 0 1",B.eQ,null,!1,"","a2 f2 f3 c3",!1,B.C)
B.P5=new A.bb("strategy.bishoppair","The bishop pair","Two bishops cover both colours and get stronger as pawns come off and diagonals open. In an open position they usually beat two knights or bishop and knight. If you have them, open the game; if your opponent has them, keep it closed or trade one off.","6k1/pp3ppp/2n2n2/8/8/2BB4/PP3PPP/6K1 w - - 0 1",B.eQ,null,!1,"c3g7 d3h7","",!1,B.C)
B.P_=new A.bb("strategy.badbishop","Good and bad bishops","A bishop blocked by its own pawns is 'bad'. White's bishop is on dark squares and so are White's centre pawns: it is little more than a big pawn. Black's bishop is on the same colour but Black's pawns are on light squares, so it moves freely. Put your pawns on the colour your bishop is not, and trade off a bad bishop when you can.","6k1/4b3/4p3/3pPp2/3P1P2/8/3B4/6K1 w - - 0 1",B.eQ,null,!1,"","d2 e7",!1,B.C)
B.a2J=new A.t("d1d8","The queens come off.","You are a rook ahead. Simplify.",B.e,null,"","")
B.a4G=new A.t("f8d8","Two rooks against one and no counterplay for Black. The win is now a matter of technique.",null,B.e,null,"","")
