B.Rx=s(["Nimzovich-Larsen","Bird","Grob","Polish opening","Benko's opening"],t.s)
B.adt=new A.cK(17,"Flank openings: b3, f4 and others",B.Rx,"White leaves the centre pawns at home and develops from the side.\n\nWhite wants: a bishop on the long diagonal aimed at the centre, and to strike with c4 or e4 once Black has committed.\n\nBlack wants: simply to take the centre that is on offer with ...d5 and ...e5 and develop behind it. Playing naturally is enough for a good game.")
B.RW=s(["Reti"],t.s)
B.adC=new A.cK(16,"R\xe9ti opening",B.RW,"White starts with Nf3, holds the centre pawns back and attacks Black's centre from the flanks with c4 and a bishop on g2.\n\nWhite wants: to make Black's centre pawns into targets rather than strengths, and to choose the pawn structure late.\n\nBlack wants: a solid centre with ...d5, supported by ...c6 or ...e6, and easy development. Do not grab the c4 pawn and try to keep it.")
B.Rl=s(["English"],t.s)
B.ady=new A.cK(15,"English opening",B.Rl,"1.c4: a Sicilian with colours reversed and an extra move. White controls d5 from the wing.\n\nWhite wants: a knight on c3, a bishop on g2 and pressure on the light squares, then to expand on the queenside with b4.\n\nBlack wants: either ...e5, playing a Sicilian a move down but soundly, or ...c5 and symmetry, or to head for a Queen's Gambit with ...e6 and ...d5.")
B.RY=s(["Sicilian"],t.s)
B.adz=new A.cK(1,"Sicilian defence",B.RY,"Black answers 1.e4 from the side with ...c5, fighting for d4 without copying White. The most popular and the sharpest reply.\n\nWhite wants: to open the game with d4, use the lead in development and attack on the kingside before Black is organised.\n\nBlack wants: after ...cxd4, an extra centre pawn and the half-open c-file. Black's counterplay is on the queenside; the longer the game goes, the better Black's structure looks.")
B.Rn=s(["French"],t.s)
B.ads=new A.cK(2,"French defence",B.Rn,"Black prepares ...d5 with ...e6. The centre often locks with white pawns on d4 and e5.\n\nWhite wants: the space on the kingside that the e5 pawn gives, and an attack there.\n\nBlack wants: to hit the pawn chain at its base with ...c5 and later ...f6. The price of the solid set-up is the bishop on c8, shut in behind e6: finding it a job is half of French strategy.")
B.Rc=s(["Caro"],t.s)
B.adp=new A.cK(3,"Caro-Kann defence",B.Rc,"The French idea with the c-pawn: ...c6 and ...d5. The bishop on c8 stays free.\n\nWhite wants: space and a small lead in development; e5 or an early c4 are the ambitious tries.\n\nBlack wants: to bring the queen's bishop out to f5 or g4 before playing ...e6, then a solid position with no weaknesses. Hard to beat, a little slow.")
B.RZ=s(["Spanish"],t.s)
B.adr=new A.cK(4,"Spanish opening (Ruy Lopez)",B.RZ,"The bishop on b5 presses the knight that defends e5.\n\nWhite wants: no quick win of the e-pawn, but lasting pressure: castle, Re1, c3 and d4 to build a full centre.\n\nBlack wants: to hold e5, chase the bishop with ...a6 and ...b5, and complete development. The main lines are among the deepest in chess; the plans matter more than the moves.")
B.Rb=s(["King's gambit"],t.s)
B.adn=new A.cK(6,"King's Gambit",B.Rb,"White offers the f-pawn on move two.\n\nWhite wants: to pull Black's e-pawn away, build a centre with d4 and attack f7 down the open f-file.\n\nBlack wants: either to take the pawn and hold it with ...g5, or to give it back at the right moment with ...d5 for free development. Both kings end up exposed: calculate, do not coast.")
B.Tz=s(["Scottish","Petrov","Philidor","King's knight","Damiano","Latvian","Greco","Brazilian","counter-gambit","Bishop's opening","Centre game","Danish","Vienna","Alapin's opening","Mengarini","Napoleon","King's head","Patzer","Lopez opening","King's pawn game"],t.s)
B.adx=new A.cK(5,"Open games: 1.e4 e5",B.Tz,"Both sides put a pawn on the fourth rank. Pieces come out fast and tactics start early.\n\nWhite wants: Nf3 hitting e5, a bishop on c4 or b5, quick castling, and then d4 to open the centre while ahead in development.\n\nBlack wants: to defend e5 with ...Nc6, develop the king's side and castle. The square to watch for both sides is f7 (f2 for White): only the king guards it.")
B.RR=s(["Pirc","Robatsch","Modern","Norwegian"],t.s)
B.ado=new A.cK(7,"Pirc and Modern defences",B.RR,"Black lets White build a full pawn centre and fianchettoes the king's bishop.\n\nWhite wants: to use the space, either quietly with Nf3 and Be2 or with a pawn storm starting f4.\n\nBlack wants: to undermine the centre later with ...e5 or ...c5, with the g7 bishop behind the break. Flexible, and risky if Black waits too long.")
B.R0=s(["Alekhine","Scandinavian"],t.s)
B.adm=new A.cK(8,"Alekhine and Scandinavian defences",B.R0,"Two ways of challenging e4 on the first move. In the Alekhine the knight tempts White's pawns forward; in the Scandinavian Black plays ...d5 at once.\n\nWhite wants: to gain time, on the knight with pawn moves or on the queen with Nc3, and keep the extra space.\n\nBlack wants: to prove the advanced pawns are targets, or simply to reach a sound Caro-Kann-like structure.")
B.RI=s(["Queen's gambit","Slav"],t.s)
B.adq=new A.cK(9,"Queen's Gambit",B.RI,"Not a real gambit: White offers the c-pawn to pull Black's d-pawn away from the centre.\n\nWhite wants: pressure on d5 with Nc3 and Bg5, and often a minority attack with the b-pawn on the queenside.\n\nBlack wants: to hold d5 with ...e6 (solid, but the c8 bishop is shut in) or ...c6 (the Slav, keeping it free), or to take on c4 and hit back with ...c5. Do not try to keep the pawn: White gets it back with e3 and Bxc4.")
B.Tn=s(["King's Indian"],t.s)
B.adl=new A.cK(10,"King's Indian defence",B.Tn,"Black lets White have pawns on c4, d4 and e4, fianchettoes and castles.\n\nWhite wants: space, and after the centre locks with d5, a queenside advance with c5.\n\nBlack wants: ...e5, and once the centre is closed, a pawn storm on the kingside with ...f5 and ...g5. The two sides attack on opposite wings; whoever gets there first wins.")
B.Ru=s(["Queen's Indian","Old Indian","Budapest"],t.s)
B.adk=new A.cK(11,"Queen's Indian, Old Indian and Budapest",B.Ru,"Defences to 1.d4 that start with ...Nf6 and hold back the d-pawn.\n\nWhite wants: the full centre with e4 if Black allows it.\n\nBlack wants: to stop e4 with pieces, the bishop from b7 in the Queen's Indian, or to hold a compact centre with ...d6 and ...e5 in the Old Indian, or to gambit a pawn for quick development in the Budapest.")
B.R5=s(["Benoni","Benko"],t.s)
B.adv=new A.cK(12,"Benoni and Benko gambit",B.R5,"Black meets d4 with ...c5 and lets the pawn advance to d5.\n\nWhite wants: the central pawn majority, and the break e5.\n\nBlack wants: play on the queenside and the long diagonal for the g7 bishop. In the Benko Black gives a pawn for open a- and b-files and pressure that lasts into the endgame.")
B.Rg=s(["Dutch"],t.s)
B.adA=new A.cK(13,"Dutch defence",B.Rg,"Black grabs e4 with the f-pawn.\n\nWhite wants: to use the weakened diagonals around Black's king, usually with g3 and Bg2.\n\nBlack wants: a kingside attack, with a knight on e4 or the pawns on f5, e6 and d5 (the Stonewall). Ambitious and loosening.")
B.St=s(["Trompovsky","Torre","London","Stonewall","Levitsky"],t.s)
B.adB=new A.cK(14,"Queen's pawn systems: London, Torre, Trompovsky",B.St,"Set-ups White can play against almost anything, with little to memorise.\n\nWhite wants: the queen's bishop outside the pawn chain on f4 or g5, then e3, c3 and a solid centre; later a kingside attack.\n\nBlack wants: to develop naturally and challenge with ...c5 and ...Qb6, hitting b2 once the bishop has left c1.")
